    {
      "id": "02",
      "title": "Which page to evict?",
      "question": "Memory is full and a page must go. Which one should the OS evict?",
      "scenes": [
        {
          "type": "question",
          "say": "Question two. Memory is full, and a page has to go. Which one should the OS evict? Pause and answer out loud.",
          "pauseAfter": 3,
          "props": {
            "question": "Memory is full. Which page <em>goes</em>?"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "points",
          "say": "The optimal choice evicts the page that will be used furthest in the future. That's impossible, since nobody knows the future, but it's the yardstick. FIFO evicts the oldest page, even if it's the hottest, and suffers Belady's anomaly: for some access patterns, more memory means more faults. Least recently used bets that the past predicts the future, and usually wins. But exact LRU means updating a list on every memory access: far too slow.",
          "props": {
            "title": "Replacement <em>policies</em>",
            "items": [
              {
                "at": "optimal",
                "label": "Optimal (MIN)",
                "title": "Furthest next use",
                "body": "Needs the future; the yardstick.",
                "code": "Belady, 1966"
              },
              {
                "at": "oldest",
                "label": "FIFO",
                "title": "Oldest page",
                "body": "Ignores use. More frames can mean more faults.",
                "code": "3 frames: 9 faults · 4 frames: 10"
              },
              {
                "at": "bets",
                "label": "LRU",
                "title": "Least recently used",
                "body": "The past predicts the future.",
                "code": "temporal locality"
              },
              {
                "at": "slow",
                "label": "Exact LRU",
                "title": "Too expensive",
                "body": "Updating a list on every access.",
                "code": "so: approximate it"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "graph",
          "say": "So real systems approximate it with the clock algorithm. The hardware sets a use bit whenever a page is accessed. Pages sit in a circle, with a hand. To evict, check the page under the hand. If its use bit is set, it was used recently: clear the bit, giving it a second chance, and move on. Page one's bit is clear, so it's the one evicted. Pages that keep getting used keep setting their bits, and survive. Linux refines this with active and inactive lists, driven by the same bits.",
          "props": {
            "title": "The <em>clock</em> algorithm",
            "nodes": [
              {
                "id": "p0",
                "label": "page 0",
                "x": 700,
                "y": 290,
                "w": 190,
                "h": 72
              },
              {
                "id": "p1",
                "label": "page 1",
                "x": 1000,
                "y": 460,
                "w": 190,
                "h": 72
              },
              {
                "id": "p2",
                "label": "page 2",
                "x": 700,
                "y": 640,
                "w": 190,
                "h": 72
              },
              {
                "id": "p3",
                "label": "page 3",
                "x": 400,
                "y": 460,
                "w": 190,
                "h": 72
              }
            ],
            "edges": [
              {
                "from": "p0",
                "to": "p1"
              },
              {
                "from": "p1",
                "to": "p2"
              },
              {
                "from": "p2",
                "to": "p3"
              },
              {
                "from": "p3",
                "to": "p0"
              }
            ],
            "panels": [
              {
                "key": "use",
                "label": "use bit",
                "type": "table",
                "initial": {}
              }
            ],
            "steps": [
              {
                "at": "accessed",
                "panels": {
                  "use": {
                    "p0": "1",
                    "p1": "0",
                    "p2": "1",
                    "p3": "0"
                  }
                },
                "note": "Hardware sets the use bit on each access"
              },
              {
                "at": "circle",
                "note": "Pages in a circle"
              },
              {
                "at": "hand",
                "nodes": {
                  "p0": "queued"
                },
                "note": "The hand points at page 0"
              },
              {
                "at": "recently",
                "nodes": {
                  "p0": "active"
                },
                "note": "page 0: use = 1, recently used"
              },
              {
                "at": "chance",
                "panels": {
                  "use": {
                    "p0": "0"
                  }
                },
                "nodes": {
                  "p0": "base"
                },
                "note": "Clear it: a second chance"
              },
              {
                "at": "move",
                "nodes": {
                  "p1": "queued"
                },
                "edges": {
                  "p0-p1": "relax"
                },
                "note": "Advance the hand"
              },
              {
                "at": "evicted",
                "nodes": {
                  "p1": "dim"
                },
                "edges": {
                  "p0-p1": "base"
                },
                "note": "page 1: use = 0 → evict"
              },
