    {
      "id": "03",
      "title": "Dirty pages and thrashing",
      "question": "Why does a machine that runs out of memory grind to a halt instead of slowing down a little?",
      "scenes": [
        {
          "type": "question",
          "say": "Question three. When a machine runs out of memory, why does it grind to a halt, instead of just slowing down a little? Pause and answer out loud.",
          "pauseAfter": 3,
          "props": {
            "question": "Out of memory: why a <em>halt</em>, not a slowdown?"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "compare",
          "say": "Evicting a clean page is free: an identical copy is already on disk, so the frame is simply reused. A dirty page has been written, so it must be written out first: a second disk I/O, on the fault path. So the kernel prefers clean pages, and writes dirty ones back in the background, before it's under pressure.",
          "props": {
            "title": "Clean or <em>dirty</em>",
            "left": {
              "at": "clean",
              "label": "Clean page",
              "title": "Free to evict",
              "tone": "good",
              "body": "Identical on disk already: just reuse the frame."
            },
            "right": {
              "at": "dirty",
              "label": "Dirty page",
              "title": "Write it out first",
              "body": "A second I/O on the fault path. The PTE's dirty bit says which."
            },
            "verdict": {
              "at": "background",
              "text": "Write dirty pages back early, in the background.",
              "code": "kswapd · writeback"
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "points",
          "say": "A process's working set is the pages it actively uses. When the working sets of everything running add up to more than RAM, every eviction throws out a page that's needed again soon. The system spends its time paging, and almost none computing. That's thrashing, and adding work makes it worse. The fixes: run fewer things, add memory, or let the out of memory killer end one process, so the rest can run.",
          "props": {
            "title": "<em>Thrashing</em>",
            "items": [
              {
                "at": "working",
                "label": "Working set",
                "title": "Pages in active use",
                "body": "What must stay resident to make progress.",
                "code": "RSS ≠ working set"
              },
              {
                "at": "needed",
                "label": "Over-commit",
                "title": "Sum > RAM",
                "body": "Every eviction is needed again soon.",
                "code": "major faults climb"
              },
              {
                "at": "thrashing",
                "label": "Thrashing",
                "title": "All paging, no work",
                "body": "More work makes it worse.",
                "code": "vmstat: si / so"
              },
              {
                "at": "fixes",
                "label": "Fixes",
                "title": "Do less, or add RAM",
                "body": "Or the OOM killer ends one process.",
                "code": "oom_score_adj"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "recap",
          "say": "To recap. A page fault traps into the kernel, which maps a frame, fetching data if needed, and retries. Minor faults need no I/O, and major faults wait for storage. The clock algorithm approximates LRU with use bits. And when working sets outgrow RAM, the machine thrashes.",
          "props": {
            "title": "Page faults, <em>in four lines</em>",
            "items": [
              {
                "at": "retries",
                "text": "Fault → map a frame (fetch if needed) → retry.",
                "code": "or SIGSEGV"
              },
              {
                "at": "Minor",
                "text": "Minor: no I/O. Major: wait for storage.",
                "code": "µs vs ms"
              },
              {
                "at": "approximates",
                "text": "Clock approximates LRU with use bits.",
                "code": "second chance"
              },
              {
                "at": "outgrow",
                "text": "Working sets > RAM → thrashing.",
                "code": "OOM killer"
              }
            ]
          }
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
